from core import subjects

def G(id,recognize,method,traps,formulas):
 l=next(l for s in subjects for l in s['lessons'] if l['id']==id)
 l['gateGuide']={'recognize':recognize,'method':[x.strip() for x in method.split('~')], 'traps':[{'claim':x.split('::',1)[0].strip(),'why':x.split('::',1)[1].strip()} for x in traps.split('||')], 'formulas':[{'latex':x.split('::',1)[0].strip(),'meaning':x.split('::',1)[1].strip()} for x in formulas.split('||')] if formulas else []}

G('0-propositions','Use truth assignments when a question asks whether an implication or argument is always valid. Use algebraic equivalence when an expression needs simplifying.',
'Mark which words express necessary or sufficient conditions before translating to symbols.~To refute validity, force every premise true and the conclusion false. If these requirements contradict, no counterexample exists.~For several options, compare their false cases instead of building four complete truth tables; verify any shortcut against the full logical conditions.',
'Affirming the consequent::From “divisible by 4 implies even” and “6 is even,” you cannot infer divisibility by 4. The conclusion adds information the premise never promised.||Treating implication as AND::With P false and Q false, P implies Q is true while P AND Q is false. Implication excludes only a broken promise with true antecedent and false consequent.',
 r'P\to Q\equiv\neg P\lor Q::The single false case is P=true,Q=false.||\neg(P\to Q)\equiv P\land\neg Q::Use this form when constructing a counterexample.||P\to Q\equiv\neg Q\to\neg P::Contrapositive, unlike converse, preserves equivalence.')
G('0-quantifiers','Quantifier questions often turn on whether one witness works universally or a different witness can depend on each input.',
'Write the domain and brackets first; an omitted scope can change what is being quantified.~For each universal choice, determine whether the existential variable can depend on it. Write an explicit witness function when possible.~To negate, swap quantifiers one at a time and negate the final predicate, including its implications or inequalities.',
'Swapping the order freely::Every integer has a larger integer, choosing y=x+1. There is no single integer larger than all integers, since choosing x=y defeats it.||Negating just the predicate::The negation of “every student passed some exam” is one student who passed no exams, not every student failing some exam.',
 r'\neg\forall x\,P(x)\equiv\exists x\,\neg P(x)::One counterexample disproves a universal claim.||\neg\exists x\,P(x)\equiv\forall x\,\neg P(x)::No witness means every candidate fails.||\neg\forall x\exists y\,R(x,y)\equiv\exists x\forall y\,\neg R(x,y)::Track quantifier order while pushing negation inward.')
G('0-sets-functions','First classify the object: set, relation or function. For a relation test each required axiom; for a function distinguish domain, image and codomain.',
'List allowed pairs or use a relation matrix for small finite domains. Do not infer transitivity from checking only consecutive-looking labels.~For injectivity compare equal outputs; for surjectivity solve for an input reaching an arbitrary codomain element.~For counting, identify independent choices. A relation from A to B chooses a subset of their Cartesian product, whereas a function chooses exactly one output per input.',
'Confusing relations with functions::With m inputs and n outputs, arbitrary relations have 2^(mn) possibilities, but total functions have n^m. A function must cover every input with exactly one output.||Antisymmetric means asymmetric::The identity relation is both symmetric and antisymmetric. Antisymmetry only rules out two opposite directed pairs between distinct elements.',
 r'|A\times B|=|A|\,|B|::Finite Cartesian-product size.||N_{\mathrm{relations}}=2^{mn},\quad N_{\mathrm{functions}}=n^m::Relations choose pairs independently; functions choose one output per input.|||A\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\cap B|::Subtract overlap to avoid double counting.')
G('0-orders-lattices','Hasse-diagram questions ask about reachability and universal bounds. A nearby-looking node is not necessarily a least or greatest bound.',
'Use only the stated order, such as divisibility or subset inclusion. Numeric size alone may be irrelevant.~Find all common upper/lower bounds by paths, then check whether a candidate is below/above every other bound.~Test every pair for the lattice property. Top and bottom establish extreme bounds but do not guarantee intermediate joins and meets.',
'Minimal means least::In {2,3,6} under divisibility, both 2 and 3 are minimal, but neither is least because they are incomparable.||Using numeric max as join::For divisors 4 and 6 of 12, the join is 12, not 6, because 6 is not divisible by 4.',
 r'a\wedge b=\gcd(a,b),\quad a\vee b=\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)::In a divisor lattice under divisibility.||A\wedge B=A\cap B,\quad A\vee B=A\cup B::In a power-set lattice under inclusion.')
G('0-algebraic-structures','Questions about groups depend on both the set and the operation. Modular addition and modular multiplication need different identities and inverse tests.',
'Check closure first, then associativity, then a two-sided identity. Commutativity is a separate property, not part of every group definition.~For each element solve the inverse equation inside the given set. One missing inverse disproves group status.~For modular multiplication use gcd(a,n)=1 to test invertibility; for element order repeatedly apply the operation until reaching identity.',
'Every nonzero residue has an inverse::Modulo 6, residue 2 cannot have a multiplicative inverse: 2x is never congruent to 1. Prime-modulus examples cannot be copied to composite moduli.||Identity is always 1::For addition modulo n the identity is 0; 1 is a multiplicative identity.',
 r'a^{-1}a=aa^{-1}=e::An inverse must return the operation’s identity.||a\text{ invertible modulo }n\iff\gcd(a,n)=1::Multiplicative modular inverse criterion.||\operatorname{ord}(a)\mid |G|::For finite groups, element order divides group size; the converse existence claim need not hold.')
G('0-graphs-matching','Separate the requested quantity: edges, components, matching size or chromatic number. The same graph drawing supports very different computations.',
'Check directed/undirected and simple/multigraph assumptions before using degree or tree formulas.~For colouring get a lower bound from cliques or odd cycles, then construct a colouring achieving an upper bound.~For maximum matching, use an augmenting path or an optimality argument; inability to add a single edge proves only maximality.',
'All greedy matchings are maximum::On path 1–2–3–4, choosing edge 2–3 gives a maximal matching of size 1. Choosing 1–2 and 3–4 gives a maximum matching of size 2.||Counting colours separately for components::Disconnected components can reuse colour names. Their union’s chromatic number is the maximum of component chromatic numbers, not their sum.',
 r'\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2|E|::Undirected handshake identity.||\omega(G)\le\chi(G)\le\Delta(G)+1::Clique lower bound and greedy degree upper bound.|||N(S)|\ge|S|\quad\forall S\subseteq X::Hall’s condition for a matching saturating bipartite part X.')
G('0-counting','Read whether order matters, repetition is allowed, and objects are labelled. Those words determine the counting model before any factorial is used.',
'Create mutually exclusive cases or an explicit construction so every object is counted exactly once.~For “at least one,” consider total minus none. For overlapping requirements use inclusion–exclusion rather than simply adding cases.~For distributing identical items, check whether empty boxes are allowed before applying stars and bars. Verify a small instance if the formula feels uncertain.',
'Dividing by factorial automatically::Repeated symbols do not create r! distinct orderings of every object. A multiset permutation needs division by the factorial of each repeated count.||At least one and exactly one::Length-4 binary strings with at least one 1 number 15; with exactly one 1 they number 4. These are different events.',
 r'\binom nr=\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!},\quad P(n,r)=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}::Unordered versus ordered selection without repetition.||N_{x_1+\cdots+x_k=n,\ x_i\ge0}=\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}::Stars and bars for nonnegative integer parts.||\frac{n!}{n_1!\cdots n_k!}::Distinct permutations of a multiset.')
G('0-recurrences','Recurrence problems require initial conditions as well as a transition. Generating-function questions require exact indexing, especially whether the sequence begins at zero.',
'Compute two or three terms to anchor the indexing and expose a wrong proposed closed form.~Choose expansion, characteristic roots or generating functions based on the recurrence form. Solve constants using every required initial condition.~Substitute the answer into both the recurrence and bases. When shifting a generating-function sum, explicitly retain the omitted starting terms.',
'Dropping the boundary term::For A(x)=sum from n=0, summing a_n x^n from n=1 gives A(x)−a_0, not A(x). Missing this term changes the entire answer.||Treating a repeated root as distinct::Two multiples of the same root sequence are linearly dependent; a double root needs an n multiplier for the second basis solution.',
 r'A(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_nx^n::Ordinary generating function, interpreted formally for counting.||\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}x^n=\frac1{1-x},\quad\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}nx^n=\frac{x}{(1-x)^2}::Two identities used to derive linear-sequence generating functions.||a_n=ua_{n-1}+va_{n-2}\Rightarrow\lambda^2-u\lambda-v=0::Characteristic equation for a homogeneous second-order recurrence.')
G('0-matrices-rank','Linear-system questions usually ask consistency, uniqueness or degrees of freedom. Matrix shape alone cannot answer these; the pivots and augmented rank can.',
'Row-reduce the coefficient matrix and right-hand side together, recording pivots and contradictory rows.~Compare coefficient and augmented ranks to decide consistency. Only then subtract rank from unknown count for free variables.~For square invertibility, determinant nonzero is a shortcut; when using elimination to compute it, account for swaps and row scaling.',
'More equations means unique solution::Three equations can repeat the same constraint. Independent pivots, not raw equation count, determine rank.||A zero coefficient row always means infinitely many solutions::If its augmented entry is nonzero, it means inconsistency; if the entry is zero it is redundancy.',
 r'\operatorname{rank}(A)=\operatorname{rank}([A\mid b])::Necessary and sufficient consistency criterion over the usual real field.||\operatorname{nullity}(A)=n-\operatorname{rank}(A)::n is the number of unknowns/columns.||\det\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}=ad-bc::Two-by-two determinant; square A is invertible iff determinant is nonzero.')
G('0-eigen-lu','Distinguish eigenvalues, eigenvector directions and a full diagonalization. Repeated eigenvalues require an eigenspace calculation rather than a yes/no shortcut.',
'Find roots of det(A−λI)=0, then solve the homogeneous system for each λ, excluding the zero vector.~Check trace and determinant against the roots. Count independent eigenvectors to determine diagonalizability.~For LU, track elimination multipliers in L; if pivoting is needed include P. Verify by multiplication before solving triangular systems.',
'Repeated root implies not diagonalizable::2I has a repeated eigenvalue and every nonzero vector is an eigenvector. The Jordan matrix with a 1 above its diagonal has only one independent direction.||LU always works without pivoting::A nonsingular matrix can have a zero initial pivot. Row permutation can be needed even when the full determinant is nonzero.',
 r'Av=\lambda v,\quad v\ne0::Eigenvector definition.||\det(A-\lambda I)=0,\quad \sum_i\lambda_i=\operatorname{tr}(A),\quad\prod_i\lambda_i=\det(A)::Characteristic equation and root checks, counting multiplicities.||PA=LU,\quad Ly=Pb,\quad Ux=y::Solve the permuted system with forward then backward substitution.')
G('0-limits-continuity','A piecewise function can have a limit, continuity and differentiability as three different outcomes. Test them in that order, at the actual boundary.',
'Compute left and right limits separately and compare. Simplify removable factors only away from the point.~For continuity require the common limit to equal the explicitly defined point value.~For differentiability compare left and right difference quotients; continuity alone is not enough. Solve parameter conditions in sequence, retaining all requirements.',
'Zero over zero equals zero::It is indeterminate. Factoring (x²−a²)/(x−a) gives x+a off the point and limit 2a, not zero.||Matching values matches derivatives::The graph of |x| meets at zero but slopes are −1 and 1. Continuity does not remove a corner.',
 r'\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)=\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)=f(a)::Continuity requires all three quantities to agree.||f^{\prime}(a)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}h::Use one-sided versions to test a corner.||\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin x}{x}=1::This standard limit assumes angles in radians.')
G('0-extrema-mvt','Extremum and MVT questions require checking their interval and hypotheses. A stationary point is only a candidate, while an endpoint can be the absolute optimum.',
'List endpoints and interior points where derivative vanishes or fails to exist; discard candidates outside the interval.~Classify local behaviour using derivative signs, then compare function values for absolute extrema.~For MVT verify continuity/differentiability first, compute the secant slope, and solve f′(c) equal to it inside the open interval.',
'f′=0 guarantees an extremum::For x³, derivative vanishes at zero but the function increases through it. The first-derivative sign does not switch.||Ignoring endpoints::x² on [1,2] has no interior stationary point but its absolute minimum and maximum exist at the endpoints.',
 r'f^{\prime}(c)=\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a},\quad a<c<b::Mean value theorem after checking its hypotheses.||f^{\prime}(c)=0,\ f^{\prime\prime}(c)>0\Rightarrow\text{local minimum}::Second-derivative test; zero second derivative is inconclusive.')
G('0-integration','Decide whether the problem asks signed integral or geometric area. For substitutions and improper endpoints, keep the transformed variable and limits explicit.',
'Choose a simplification: elementary antiderivative, substitution, integration by parts or symmetry. State why its conditions apply.~Write an antiderivative or transformed integral on its own line; change both limits if using a new variable.~Evaluate upper minus lower, inspect signs and units, and use actual one-sided limits at singularities or infinity.',
'Signed integral is always area::The integral of x from −1 to 1 is zero, but geometric area is 1 because negative and positive areas both count positively.||Symmetry resolves a singular integral::The ordinary improper integral of 1/x from −1 to 1 does not exist. A symmetric principal value is a different definition and cannot be silently substituted.',
 r'\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a)::For an appropriate antiderivative and a well-defined integral.||\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du::Integration by parts.||\int_{-a}^{a}f(x)\,dx=0\quad(f\text{ odd and integrable})::Check integrability before using cancellation.')
G('0-conditional-bayes','Conditioning changes the denominator. Disease tests, manufacturing sources and urn selections become clearer when all ways to obtain the observed evidence are counted.',
'Define the cause classes and write their priors; state which event is already known.~Compute evidence probability by total probability across every class, including false positives or alternative sources.~Use Bayes to reverse the condition. Check that posterior is between zero and one and that plausible evidence changes the prior in the expected direction.',
'High sensitivity gives high posterior::With a rare condition, false positives among the much larger healthy group can outnumber true positives. Posterior also depends on prevalence.||Using independent probabilities without justification::Two draws without replacement change the population; the second factor must be conditional on the first.',
 r'P(A\mid B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)},\quad P(B)>0::Conditional probability restricts the sample population to B.||P(B_j\mid A)=\frac{P(A\mid B_j)P(B_j)}{\sum_iP(A\mid B_i)P(B_i)}::Bayes with a partition of possible causes.||P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)\quad\text{if independent}::Disjointness is not independence.')
G('0-random-statistics','Moment questions distinguish E[X²] from E[X]². Continuous-density questions require normalization before expectation or interval probability.',
'Check the PMF sums or PDF integrates to one; solve any unknown normalization constant first.~Compute first and second moments separately, then variance. For transformed variables use linearity and the square scaling rule.~For data summaries sort before finding median, and check whether the variance requested is population or an unbiased sample estimator.',
'E[X²]=E[X]²::For a fair variable taking 0 or 2, these are 2 and 1 respectively. Their difference is the nonzero variance.||Adding a constant changes variance::It shifts all values and the mean equally, so deviations are unchanged. Scaling by a multiplies variance by a², even if a is negative.',
 r'\mathbb E[X]=\sum_xxp(x),\quad\mathbb E[X^2]=\sum_xx^2p(x)::Discrete first and second moments.||\operatorname{Var}(X)=\mathbb E[X^2]-\mathbb E[X]^2::Variance is never negative for a valid distribution.||\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\operatorname{Var}(X)::Translations leave spread unchanged.')
G('0-binomial-poisson','Match the experiment to the distribution: fixed independent equal-p trials for binomial; event counts with a specified window rate for Poisson.',
'Write n,p or the actual window mean λ before plugging in a mass formula.~For at least one event, compute one minus zero instead of summing every positive count.~For independent counts use indicators or distribution addition, but treat Poisson approximation as an approximation and retain the correct time-unit conversion.',
'Poisson rate equals mean for every window::A rate of 6 per hour has mean 2 in 20 minutes. The rate must be multiplied by the duration in consistent units.||Any sequence of trials is binomial::Changing success probabilities or dependence invalidates the elementary binomial model even when outcomes are successes/failures.',
 r'P(X=k)=\binom nkp^k(1-p)^{n-k}::Binomial mass for k=0,…,n.||\mathbb E[X]=np,\quad\operatorname{Var}(X)=np(1-p)::Binomial moments.||P(Y=k)=e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^k}{k!},\quad\lambda=rt::Poisson mass with mean/variance λ for a duration t.')
G('0-continuous-distributions','Read whether a parameter is rate, variance or standard deviation. Continuous probabilities concern intervals rather than density heights.',
'Identify support and parameterization, then choose CDF/survival, interval fraction or normal standardization.~Transform event bounds as well as variables. If a scale factor is negative, reverse the inequality.~Inspect edge cases: probabilities outside support, symmetric normal half-probabilities, and the reciprocal relation between exponential rate and mean.',
'Dividing by normal variance::Normal(10,4) has σ=2, so X=14 standardizes to 2, not 1.||Density is a point probability::A uniform density can be greater than 1 on a short interval while all probabilities remain at most 1. Integrating over an interval gives probability.',
 r'P(c<X<d)=\frac{\text{length}((c,d)\cap[a,b])}{b-a}::Uniform[a,b] probability includes support overlap.||P(X>t)=e^{-\lambda t},\quad\mathbb E[X]=\frac1\lambda::Exponential with rate λ and t≥0.||Z=\frac{X-\mu}{\sigma},\quad P(X\le x)=\Phi\!\left(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\right)::Normal standardization uses standard deviation.')
G('1-boolean','Boolean minimization questions reward removing redundant terms while preserving every truth-table row. Canonical and minimal forms are different outputs.',
'Expand or factor with Boolean identities, not ordinary subtraction. Establish whether SOP or POS is requested.~Use absorption and complement pairs before expanding everything; fewer terms often expose the essential variable.~Check the simplified form on a discriminating assignment, especially rows where eliminated literals change.',
'Using arithmetic cancellation::A+A=A, not 2A. Likewise A+AB=A because AB adds no accepting row beyond A.||Minimal and canonical mean the same::A canonical minterm names every variable; a minimal implicant intentionally omits changing variables.',
 r'A+AB=A,\quad AB+A\bar B=A::Absorption and combining complementary cases.||\overline{AB}=\bar A+\bar B::De Morgan swaps AND and OR.')
G('1-kmap-tabular','Read whether unspecified minterms are zeros or don’t-cares. A map group must respect Gray-code adjacency and required-zero exclusions.',
'Write minterms in binary and label rows/columns in Gray order.~Find largest valid power-of-two rectangles, including wraparound, then identify essential prime implicants.~Cover remaining required ones economically. Don’t-cares can enlarge a group but never require a group by themselves.',
'Diagonal cells are adjacent::A diagonal can change two input bits, so combining it does not eliminate just one literal.||All don’t-cares must be covered::They represent unconstrained outputs. Covering unnecessary ones can make the implementation larger instead of smaller.',
 r'\text{remaining literals}=n-\log_2g::A group of g=2^k cells in an n-variable map eliminates k changing variables.')
G('1-combinational','Translate the specification into output functions first. A multiplexer is a function implementation tool, not merely a selector to memorize.',
'Build the truth table and separate each output, such as sum and carry.~For a mux choose select variables and substitute each selector combination to find residual data-input functions.~For delay, trace the longest dependent gate path rather than adding delays of independent parallel branches.',
'XOR and OR are interchangeable in an adder::For inputs 1,1, a half-adder sum is 0, while OR would give 1. Carry separately represents the value 2.||Mux inputs must be constants::Residual functions can be B, its complement, or other available signals when only some variables are selectors.',
 r'S=A\oplus B\oplus C_{in},\quad C_{out}=AB+AC_{in}+BC_{in}::Full-adder outputs.||Y=\bar S I_0+S I_1::Two-to-one mux equation.')
G('1-sequential','Distinguish present state, next state and outputs. Timing questions also distinguish edge-triggered storage from a transparent latch.',
'Write a state/transition table with input conditions before assigning binary encodings.~Use the chosen flip-flop’s characteristic or excitation rule to determine required inputs.~Include reset and unused encodings. For timing, combine clock-to-Q, combinational path and setup requirements under the specified model.',
'Every unused state is harmless::A counter can become trapped in an unused encoding unless recovery transitions are defined.||State count equals flip-flop count::k binary flip-flops encode up to 2^k states, not k states.',
 r'Q^+=D,\quad Q^+=Q\oplus T::D and T flip-flop next-state rules.||k=\lceil\log_2n\rceil::Minimum binary state bits for n states.||T_{clk}\ge t_{cq}+t_{comb}+t_{setup}::Simplified synchronous timing bound, without additional skew/jitter assumptions.')
G('1-representation','Identify width, signedness and fractional scaling before interpreting a bit pattern. Floating-point exponents require special-case handling.',
'Decode the sign and bit weights at the specified width. For two’s complement a leading 1 contributes a negative high-bit weight.~For fixed point divide the stored signed/unsigned integer by 2^f. For IEEE normals reconstruct the implicit leading bit and unbiased exponent.~Check overflow using the correct signed or unsigned rule; round only according to the stated format.',
'Carry-out equals signed overflow::4-bit 1111+0001 has carry-out but represents −1+1=0 without signed overflow. 0111+0001 gives a negative pattern and signed overflow without carry-out.||Implicit leading 1 is universal::Subnormal IEEE values use a different leading-significand convention than normal values.',
 r'-2^{n-1}\le x\le2^{n-1}-1::Signed two’s-complement range.||x=\frac{I}{2^f}::Fixed-point scaling.||x=(-1)^s(1.f)_2\,2^{E-127}::IEEE single-precision normal value; reserved exponent fields require separate rules.')
G('2-instructions','Performance questions combine instruction count, instruction mix, CPI and frequency. A clock-rate comparison alone is incomplete.',
'Compute effective addresses in the machine’s addressing units, including index scaling by element size.~Find weighted CPI using instruction fractions, then total cycles.~Divide by clock frequency and convert units at the end; compare old/new times for speedup.',
'Lower CPI always means faster::A design with CPI 1 at 1 GHz is slower per instruction than CPI 1.5 at 2 GHz; instruction counts may also differ.||Index means byte displacement::Array index 3 for 4-byte elements means displacement 12 bytes, not 3.',
 r'T=\frac{IC\times CPI}{f},\quad CPI=\sum_i p_i CPI_i::CPU time and weighted average cycles/instruction.||EA=base+index\times elementSize+displacement::For the specified scaled base/index addressing form.')
G('2-alu-control','Trace an instruction as register transfers and control choices. Separate datapath work from the sequencing that selects it.',
'List operands and destination, then identify the ALU operation, mux choices and write enables.~For subtraction use the fixed-width complement-plus-one construction and inspect flags separately.~For microcode storage multiply word count by control-word width; sequencing logic is separate from stored bits.',
'Microinstructions equal program instructions::A machine instruction may require many internal microinstructions. They are different abstraction levels.||Signed comparison uses result sign alone::Wrapped signed subtraction can overflow, so sign without overflow information can give the wrong comparison.',
 r'A-B=A+\bar B+1\pmod{2^n}::Fixed-width subtraction through an adder.||\text{store bytes}=\frac{N_{words}\,W_{bits}}8::Control-memory capacity excluding additional hardware.')
G('2-cache','Address-bit questions require capacity, block size, associativity and address width. AMAT questions require a clear miss-penalty convention.',
'Convert capacity and block size to bytes, compute lines, then divide by ways to get sets.~Allocate low offset bits, then set index bits, and use remaining high bits for tag.~For access time distinguish additional miss penalty from total miss service time. If multilevel, identify local versus global miss rates.',
'Use total lines for index bits in every cache::A four-way cache has one quarter as many sets as lines. Indexing lines instead of sets steals two bits incorrectly from the tag.||Double-count hit time::If miss penalty is additional cost, hit time is already paid for every access and is added once.',
 r'S=\frac{C}{BW},\quad b=\log_2B,\quad i=\log_2S,\quad t=A-b-i::Byte-addressed cache: capacity C, block bytes B, ways W, address bits A.||AMAT=T_h+mP_m::Additional miss penalty convention.')
G('2-memory-io','Memory-chip construction separates depth from width. I/O questions compare CPU service overhead and shared-bus transfer, not just labels.',
'Expand width with parallel data lanes and depth with selected banks; determine address bits from location count.~For interrupts count handler invocations and any per-event work under the model.~For DMA include setup/completion costs and bus occupancy; controller movement does not eliminate physical transfer time.',
'DMA means the CPU pays no cost::The CPU configures transfers and handles completion, and DMA can compete for memory bandwidth.||Chip count depends only on bytes::Two organizations with the same byte capacity may need different bank/wiring arrangements when depth and width are specified.',
 r'N_{chips}=\frac{D_{target}}{D_{chip}}\frac{W_{target}}{W_{chip}}::For integral compatible depth/width expansion.||a=\log_2D::Address bits for D locations, when D is a power of two.')
G('2-pipeline','Pipeline timing requires fill, steady-state work and actual hazards. Read whether stage delays are equal and whether forwarding is available.',
'Find the clock period from the slowest stage plus register overhead; do not use average stage delay.~Count ideal fill/drain cycles, then add specified nonoverlapping stalls/flushes.~For branch CPI multiply branch frequency, conditional misprediction rate and penalty; a dependency stalls only if data cannot arrive on time.',
'Every dependency causes one stall::Forwarding can remove stalls for ALU chains, while a load-use pair may still require waiting under the timing model.||Throughput improvement means equal latency reduction::An instruction still traverses every stage; pipelining overlaps different instructions rather than skipping stages.',
 r'C_{ideal}=k+n-1,\quad T=C_{actual}T_{clk}::Initially empty k-stage ideal pipeline for n instructions.||\Delta CPI=f_b p_{miss}P_b::Expected branch penalty with nonoverlapping modelled costs.')
G('3-c-memory','Do not assign a numerical output to undefined behaviour. First determine types, storage lifetime and which object each pointer references.',
'Create a trace table for variables, types, values and pointer targets; use the stated machine-size assumptions.~Evaluate promotions, integer division and short-circuiting before assignment conversions.~Check bounds and lifetime at every dereference, and sequencing for competing updates. Undefined behaviour stops a unique-output analysis.',
'Assignment to double changes earlier division::For double x=7/2, integer division already produced 3, then conversion yields 3.0.||sizeof on an array parameter gives caller array size::The parameter is adjusted to a pointer. Actual array scope and adjusted parameter scope differ.',
 r'address(a[i])=base+i\,sizeof(T)::Contiguous array addressing in bytes.||p+j\text{ advances by }j\,sizeof(*p)\text{ bytes}::Only within the permitted object/one-past range.')
G('3-recursion','Separate descent from return-time work. Trace invocations, not just function names, because each call has its own frame.',
'Write the base case and decreasing measure, then draw the active call chain or tree.~Execute statements before recursion on descent and after recursion while unwinding.~Count total call/work nodes for time, but only the maximum simultaneous active chain for stack space.',
'Call count equals stack depth::A branching recursion may make many sequential calls while retaining only one root-to-leaf chain at a time.||A recursive formula guarantees termination::If arguments do not decrease to a valid base case, it may loop or exhaust stack rather than compute the intended sequence.',
 r'T(n)=T(n-1)+O(1)\Rightarrow T(n)=O(n)::Linear-depth, constant-work-per-frame recurrence.||S(n)=O(h\,s_{frame})::Stack use follows maximum depth h and frame size.')
G('3-linear-structures','Complexity depends on whether a position or predecessor is already known. Stack/queue traces depend on removal order and empty/full conventions.',
'Identify representation and which pointers/indices are maintained.~Draw affected links before mutating a list, or trace head/tail modulo capacity for a ring.~For expressions, preserve operand order on popping; for queues check the last-removal case resets both pointers.',
'Linked insertion is always O(1)::Finding the kth node first takes O(n); only insertion after an already-known node is constant time.||Stack subtraction pops left operand first::For postfix a b −, pop b as the right operand and a as the left; reversing gives the wrong sign.',
 r'next(i)=(i+1)\bmod N::Circular-array index update.||C_{usable}=N-1::Only for the convention that reserves one slot to distinguish full from empty.')
G('3-trees-bst','Read tree shape definitions and height convention. A BST invariant enables search but does not imply balance.',
'Trace preorder/inorder/postorder by the position of the root visit relative to child calls.~For BST updates follow comparisons; handle leaf, one-child and two-child deletion separately.~Express operation cost in height h, then apply any balance guarantee. Distinct-key assumptions matter for reconstruction and ordering.',
'BST search is always logarithmic::Ascending insertions can create a chain and linear-height search.||Complete means full::A complete tree fills levels left to right; a full tree requires every internal node have two children. Neither definition can substitute for the other.',
 r'N_{perfect}=2^{h+1}-1::Edge-height h convention for a perfect binary tree.||T_{BST}=O(h)::Search/insertion/deletion costs before assuming balance.')
G('3-heaps-graphs','A heap orders ancestors relative to descendants, not every key globally. A graph representation should match the operation being analyzed.',
'Use the indexing convention before applying parent/child formulas.~Trace append-and-bubble or root-replace-and-sift while maintaining completeness and heap order.~For graphs distinguish edge membership from neighbour enumeration, and count undirected list entries twice.',
'Heap inorder gives sorted keys::Heaps are not BSTs. Siblings and separate subtrees need not be ordered against each other.||Build-heap is n times worst sift cost::Most nodes have tiny height. Summing per-height work gives linear bottom-up construction.',
 r'left(i)=2i+1,\quad right(i)=2i+2::Zero-based binary heap child indices.||S_{list}=\Theta(V+E),\quad S_{matrix}=\Theta(V^2)::Standard graph-space bounds.')
G('4-analysis','Identify input size, measured operation and worst/average/amortized case before simplifying growth. A recursion depth is not a total-time bound.',
'Count actual loop ranges, using summation for dependent inner bounds.~Draw recursion levels or solve a recurrence, keeping combine work and branching.~For space count simultaneously live allocations and recursive frames, excluding or including input storage as requested.',
'Nested loops always give n²::An inner loop may halve, depend on i, or have a constant range; count its actual operations.||Numeric n and bit length n are interchangeable::A capacity-W dynamic program can be polynomial in W but exponential in the bits needed to encode W.',
 r'\sum_{i=1}^{n}i=\frac{n(n+1)}2::Triangular loop count.||T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)::Compare f with n^(log_b a) only when the theorem’s assumptions hold.')
G('4-search-sort','Sorting questions can ask stability, auxiliary space or worst-case time. A correct answer about one property does not answer the others.',
'For binary search state the active inclusive/exclusive bounds and prove each failed comparison shrinks them.~For sorting write the invariant, such as a sorted prefix or two sorted halves being merged.~Separate expected and worst-case quicksort; count comparisons/moves under the particular algorithm, not a universal sorting formula.',
'Randomized quicksort guarantees n log n worst case::Randomization improves expected performance but extreme partitions remain possible.||A sorted output implies stable sorting::Equal keys can be reordered while numerical key order remains correct.',
 r'T_{merge}=\Theta(n\log n),\quad T_{quick,worst}=\Theta(n^2)::Common comparison-sort bounds.||\Omega(n\log n)::General comparison sorting worst-case lower bound under the standard model.')
G('4-hashing','Collision resolution and load factor are part of the cost model. Trace the entire probe sequence, including wraparound and tombstones.',
'Compute initial hash, then apply the specified probing formula until a legal slot or search stop is reached.~Distinguish never-used empty from deleted/tombstone entries.~For complexity use distribution assumptions and load factor. Rehash entries after resizing because their destinations depend on capacity.',
'A deleted slot always terminates search::That can hide keys stored later in the same probe chain; tombstones preserve continuation.||Expected O(1) means every lookup is O(1)::Adversarial collisions or high load can cause long chains/probes; worst-case can be linear.',
 r'\alpha=\frac nm::Load factor for n keys and m buckets/slots.||h_i(k)=(h(k)+i)\bmod m::Linear probing; other schemes have different coverage conditions.')
G('4-greedy-divide','Before using greedy, identify a safe-choice argument. Before using divide-and-conquer, identify independent subproblems and combine cost.',
'State the choice or partition exactly, including tie handling.~For greedy try an exchange argument or a small counterexample; for divide-and-conquer derive a recurrence.~Check that residual instances retain the same assumptions, such as fractional versus indivisible items.',
'Greedy local benefit proves global optimum::Ratio greedy gives value 160 instead of 220 in the standard capacity-50 0/1 example, even though each early choice looks attractive.||Split count alone gives complexity::Combining sorted halves is linear work; omitting it changes merge-sort analysis.',
 r'T(n)=2T(n/2)+\Theta(n)\Rightarrow\Theta(n\log n)::Balanced divide-and-linear-combine recurrence.')
G('4-dynamic','A DP table needs an exact state meaning and dependency order. Memoization is useful only when subproblems overlap.',
'Define what dp[i,w] or an equivalent state contains before writing transitions.~Enumerate every allowed last decision, include base cases and process dependencies first.~Count reachable states times transition cost; when compressing storage, verify iteration direction preserves old-state values.',
'Upward capacities are harmless for 0/1 knapsack::With one item weight 2/value 3, an upward update can produce value 6 at capacity 4 by reusing the same item.||DP optimal value uniquely determines selected items::Different choices may attain the same optimum; reconstruction needs recorded choices or table comparisons.',
 r'dp[i,w]=\max\{dp[i-1,w],v_i+dp[i-1,w-w_i]\}::0/1 knapsack include term only when w_i≤w.||T=\#states\times cost_{transition}::State-based complexity; O(nW) is pseudopolynomial in numeric capacity.')
G('4-graph-algorithms','Match the algorithm to the graph: direction, weights, connectivity and representation change both correctness and cost.',
'List graph assumptions and the output required: reachability, distances, ordering or a spanning tree.~Select the algorithm whose invariant proves that output and trace its queue, stack or priority queue.~Count vertices and edges under the given representation; check disconnected components separately.',
'A shortest-path tree is always a minimum spanning tree::A triangle with weights 2, 2 and 3 can have a shortest-path tree of weight 5 from one endpoint while the MST has weight 4.||An adjacency matrix scans only existing edges::Checking every possible neighbor costs V per vertex even in a sparse graph.',
 r'T_{BFS}=\Theta(V+E)::Adjacency-list traversal including initialization.||T_{matrix}=\Theta(V^2)::Full traversal with an adjacency matrix.')
G('4-bfs-dfs','BFS explores distance layers; DFS explores one unfinished branch. Record discovery state at the correct time.',
'Mark a vertex discovered when enqueuing it in BFS, then record its parent and distance.~For DFS maintain discovery/finish times and an active recursion stack.~For directed cycle detection use back edges to active ancestors; for undirected graphs exclude the parent edge.',
'BFS finds shortest weighted paths::A direct edge of weight 10 loses to a two-edge path of total weight 2, although BFS prefers fewer edges.||An edge to any visited vertex proves a directed cycle::Edges to finished vertices can be cross or forward edges; a back edge to an active vertex proves a cycle.',
 r'd[v]=d[u]+1::BFS discovery via u in an unweighted graph.||d[u]<d[v]<f[v]<f[u]::DFS interval nesting when v is a descendant of u.')
G('4-minimum-spanning','MST questions concern a connected undirected weighted graph. Cut and cycle properties are safer than guessing from a picture.',
'For Kruskal sort edges and accept an edge only when it joins distinct components.~For Prim choose the cheapest edge crossing from the current tree to an outside vertex.~To discuss uniqueness inspect equal-weight alternatives; distinguish edges in some MST from edges in every MST.',
'Every minimum edge on a cut is in every MST::Ties permit alternatives; a uniquely lightest cut edge is compulsory.||Distinct weights are necessary for uniqueness::A graph that is already a tree has a unique spanning tree even when weights repeat.',
 r'|E_T|=V-1::A spanning tree connects all V vertices without a cycle.||w(T)=\sum_{e\in E_T}w(e)::MST minimizes total weight, not distances from a root.')
G('4-shortest-paths','Check negative edges before choosing Dijkstra. Shortest paths and reachable negative cycles require different conclusions.',
'Initialize source distance to zero and others to infinity; relax only reachable edges.~Use BFS for unit weights, Dijkstra for nonnegative weights, Bellman–Ford when negative weights are allowed, or a topological order for a DAG.~Use one additional Bellman–Ford pass to detect a reachable negative cycle; state which destinations are affected.',
'Dijkstra fails only if there is a negative cycle::A single negative edge can invalidate finalized distances without any cycle.||An unreachable negative cycle invalidates all source distances::Only cycles reachable from the source and able to reach the destination affect that destination.',
 r'd[v]\leftarrow\min(d[v],d[u]+w(u,v))::Relaxation, with d[u] finite.||D^{(k)}_{ij}=\min(D^{(k-1)}_{ij},D^{(k-1)}_{ik}+D^{(k-1)}_{kj})::Floyd–Warshall permits vertex k as an intermediate.')
G('5-automata','Automata questions often ask whether two descriptions recognize the same language. Acceptance depends on the entire input.',
'State a meaning for every state, then trace transitions including epsilon closures.~For NFA-to-DFA construction treat each DFA state as a set of NFA states and mark it accepting if it intersects final states.~For minimization first remove unreachable states, then distinguish accepting/nonaccepting states and refine transitions.',
'An NFA accepts only when every branch accepts::Existence of one accepting branch after consuming the entire input is sufficient.||A rejecting DFA transition can be omitted when complementing::Complement requires a complete DFA; missing transitions must lead to a rejecting sink before final states are flipped.',
 r'\delta_D(S,a)=\varepsilon\text{-closure}\!\left(\bigcup_{q\in S}\delta_N(q,a)\right)::Subset transition including epsilon moves.|||Q_D|\le 2^{|Q_N|}::Upper bound on subset states; many may be unreachable.')
G('5-regular-pumping','The pumping lemma is a necessary condition for regularity. Its order of quantifiers decides who chooses the decomposition.',
'Assume regularity and let the adversary choose pumping length p.~Choose a word depending on p, then cover every legal split with |xy|≤p and |y|>0.~Select a pumping exponent that forces departure from the language and explicitly explain why it fails.',
'One bad decomposition disproves regularity::For a regular language some legal decomposition must work; to contradict the lemma all legal decompositions must fail for your chosen word.||Passing a few pumping examples proves regularity::The lemma does not provide a sufficient test; a DFA, regular expression or closure construction can prove regularity.',
 r'\exists p\ \forall w\in L, |w|\ge p:\ \exists x,y,z\ \forall i\ge0, xy^iz\in L::Regular-language pumping quantifiers, with w=xyz, |xy|≤p and |y|>0.')
G('5-cfg-pda','A CFG generates strings by derivation; a PDA uses an unbounded stack. Write the language before designing either representation.',
'Identify the dependency, such as matching counts or nested delimiters.~Trace productions or stack operations while recording consumed input and remaining obligation.~For ambiguity produce one string with two distinct parse trees or leftmost derivations; use grammar structure to enforce precedence.',
'Two derivation orders always prove ambiguity::Expanding independent nonterminals in different orders can give the same parse tree; use distinct trees or distinct leftmost derivations.||Every context-free language has a deterministic PDA::Nondeterministic PDAs are more expressive; deterministic context-free languages form a proper subclass.',
 r'S\to aSb\mid\varepsilon::Generates equal numbers of a then b, including the empty word.||L=\{a^nb^n:n\ge0\}::A stack counts the first block and matches the second.')
G('5-cfl-pumping','For context-free pumping two substrings are pumped together within a bounded window. Track which blocks that window can touch.',
'Assume context-freeness and choose a long word with separated dependencies.~Cover all legal uvxyz decompositions with |vxy|≤p and |vy|>0.~Show some exponent changes the required count relation or block ordering, regardless of which legal split was selected.',
'The two pumped substrings must both be nonempty::Only their combined length must be positive; one can be empty.||CFLs are closed under intersection with CFLs::Intersect {a^n b^n c^m} with {a^m b^n c^n} to obtain the non-CFL {a^n b^n c^n}; intersection with a regular language is closed.',
 r'uv^ixy^iz\in L\quad(i\ge0)::CFL pumping form; |vxy|≤p and |vy|>0.')
G('5-decidability','Distinguish always halting, accepting eventually, and possibly looping. Reductions transfer impossibility in one direction.',
'Classify the machine behavior on both membership and nonmembership inputs.~To prove B undecidable, reduce a known undecidable A to B by a computable mapping.~For language operations construct recognizers explicitly; dovetail simulations when waiting sequentially could loop.',
'A recognizer decides its language::It may run forever on nonmembers; a decider must halt on all inputs.||A reduction from B to undecidable A proves B undecidable::The useful direction is A to B; otherwise B may be easier than A.',
 r'A\le_m B:\ x\in A\iff f(x)\in B::Computable mapping; a decider for B would decide A.||L\text{ and }\overline L\text{ recognizable}\Rightarrow L\text{ decidable}::Dovetail both recognizers; exactly one eventually accepts.')
G('6-lexical','Lexical analysis groups characters into tokens. Maximal munch and rule priority are separate decisions.',
'Find the longest prefix matching any token rule.~If multiple rules match that same longest prefix, use the stated priority, often rule order.~Return token kind and any needed lexeme attribute; whitespace/comments may be discarded while updating position.',
'The first rule always wins::A later rule with a longer match beats an earlier shorter match; priority resolves equal lengths.||A keyword embedded in an identifier becomes a keyword token::Under typical maximal munch, ifx is a single identifier, not if followed by x.',
 r'Token=(kind,attribute)::Attribute may carry a symbol-table reference or literal value.')
G('6-parsing','Predictive parsing uses FIRST/FOLLOW; LR parsing uses viable prefixes and item states. Label the parser family before building a table.',
'Compute nullable symbols first, then FIRST and FOLLOW to a fixed point.~For A→α place it under FIRST(α) terminals; if α is nullable also use FOLLOW(A).~For LR trace closure/goto and the stated lookahead rules; identify the exact cell with multiple actions.',
'FIRST(AB) is always FIRST(A)::If A is nullable, terminals from FIRST(B) also matter.||An unambiguous grammar is always LL(1)::Left-recursive unambiguous expression grammars still fail predictive parsing; ambiguity and parser suitability are different properties.',
 r'FIRST(\alpha)\setminus\{\varepsilon\}\subseteq Predict(A\to\alpha)::Nullable α additionally uses FOLLOW(A).||\$\in FOLLOW(S)::End marker follows the start symbol.')
G('6-translation-runtime','Keep compile-time attribute dependencies separate from runtime storage. Activation records represent individual calls, including recursive calls.',
'Classify each attribute as synthesized or inherited and draw its dependency edges.~Choose an evaluation order consistent with those edges; relate S-attributed definitions to bottom-up evaluation.~For runtime access trace lexical nesting through static links and callers through dynamic links; account for separate recursive frames.',
'Static links always point to the caller::They point to the lexically enclosing environment; the caller can be a different procedure.||One variable name means one runtime memory cell::Each recursive activation can have its own locals and parameters.',
 r'address=base+offset::Access within a frame; finding the correct base may require following static links.')
G('6-ir-dataflow','Optimization is legal only when it preserves observable behavior. Data-flow questions ask for a fixed point over a control-flow graph.',
'Write GEN/KILL or USE/DEF for each block from the stated analysis.~Identify whether information flows forward or backward and whether the meet is union or intersection.~Iterate boundary conditions and transfer equations until stable; inspect paths and aliasing before deleting or moving computations.',
'An expression repeated in source is always reusable::A reassignment to an operand or an aliased write can invalidate its previous value.||Liveness describes whether a variable has a current value::It asks whether that value may be read before being overwritten on a future path.',
 r'OUT[B]=\bigcup_{S\in succ(B)}IN[S]::Live-variable successor meet.||IN[B]=USE[B]\cup(OUT[B]\setminus DEF[B])::Backward liveness transfer.')
G('7-processes','Separate a process resource container from a thread execution context. fork creates another execution path; exec replaces an image.',
'Draw process creation as a tree and track which processes reach each later statement.~List resources that are copied, shared or replaced under the stated API.~For interleavings enforce each thread’s program order and synchronization edges, then enumerate possible outcomes.',
'Every fork doubles the original process count regardless of control flow::Only processes that actually execute that fork create a child; branches and failures change the count.||Context switching and entering kernel mode are identical::A system call can enter the kernel and return to the same process without a scheduler switch.',
 r'N=2^k::k unconditional successful fork calls reached by every existing process, with no early exits.')
G('7-synchronization','Model the shared state and allowed interleavings before choosing a lock or semaphore. Atomicity applies only to the specified operation.',
'Split non-atomic updates into read, compute and write events.~Check mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting independently.~For semaphore code trace counts, blocked queues and ordering of wait/signal; verify that the protected invariant holds after every step.',
'A single source-level increment is atomic::Two threads can read the same old value and overwrite each other’s increments.||Mutual exclusion guarantees bounded waiting::A lock can prevent simultaneous entry while repeatedly starving one contender.',
 r'wait(S):\ \text{acquire one permit or block}::Atomic semaphore operation; implementation conventions for negative counts vary.||signal(S):\ \text{return one permit/wake a waiter}::A counting semaphore permits multiple concurrent holders when initialized above one.')
G('7-deadlock','Deadlock, unsafe state and starvation are distinct. Banker’s safety test uses maximum claims and hypothetical completions.',
'Compute Need=Max−Allocation and copy Available into Work.~Repeatedly find an unfinished process whose Need≤Work, then add its current Allocation to Work.~A complete sequence proves safety; for requests first test request≤Need and request≤Available, simulate and rerun safety.',
'Unsafe means currently deadlocked::An unsafe state lacks a guaranteed safe completion sequence; actual requests may still allow everyone to finish.||A cycle always proves deadlock with multiple instances::Cycle sufficiency applies to single-instance resource types; multiple instances require a stronger analysis.',
 r'Need_{ij}=Max_{ij}-Allocation_{ij}::Remaining declared claim.||Work\leftarrow Work+Allocation_i::Release currently held resources on hypothetical completion; do not add Max.')
G('7-scheduling','A timeline is safer than mental averages. Arrival time, preemption, quantum and tie-breaking determine the order.',
'Create a Gantt chart from arrival/completion/quantum events.~For each process calculate completion minus arrival, then subtract actual CPU service to get waiting for CPU-only examples.~For response time use the first dispatch, not final completion; include context-switch cost only when specified.',
'Waiting time equals first-start minus arrival under preemption::A process may wait again after being preempted; that difference is response time.||Round robin changes only total completion time::The quantum changes dispatch order, response and waiting, even when total service is unchanged.',
 r'T_i=C_i-A_i::Turnaround time.||W_i=T_i-B_i::Waiting for a CPU-only burst B; general workloads require accounting for I/O time.||R_i=F_i-A_i::Response time from first execution F.')
G('7-virtual-files','Address translation, page faults and replacement are different stages. Identify what the question includes in access time.',
'Split the virtual address into page number and offset from the page size.~For TLB timing draw hit and miss paths, counting page-table accesses and data accesses separately.~For replacement simulate each reference, update the exact policy state and include initial empty frames.',
'A TLB miss always means a page fault::The page may be resident and its translation simply absent from the TLB.||Adding frames never increases faults::Belady’s anomaly can occur with FIFO; stack algorithms such as LRU avoid it.',
 r'VA=VPN\cdot P+offset::Page size P bytes and 0≤offset<P.||EAT=h(t_{TLB}+t_M)+(1-h)(t_{TLB}+2t_M)::Serial TLB with one-level page table, resident pages and one data access.||E=(1-p)t_{normal}+pt_{fault}::Fault time must include any restart cost specified by the problem.')
G('7-files','File allocation and disk scheduling have different objectives. State pointer sizes, block sizes and disk direction explicitly.',
'For indexed files count data pointers reached through direct, single-indirect and further levels.~For disk scheduling trace head positions and count movement under the given service/return convention.~For allocation compare random access, fragmentation and growth; include metadata blocks when counting I/O or space.',
'A single-indirect pointer stores one data block::It addresses an index block containing multiple data-block pointers.||C-SCAN wraparound is always free::Some textbook conventions omit it, others count physical movement; follow the question’s convention.',
 r'p=\left\lfloor\frac{block\ size}{pointer\ size}\right\rfloor::Pointers per index block, ignoring reserved metadata unless stated.||Capacity=B(d+p+p^2+p^3)::d direct plus one single, double and triple indirect pointer, with data-block size B.')
G('8-er-relations','ER cardinality constraints and relational keys must be derived from the scenario. Tables implement entities and relationships.',
'Identify entity keys and relationship cardinalities/participation.~Map many-to-many relationships to their own relation; place suitable foreign keys for one-to-many.~For weak entities combine owner key and partial key; verify whether relationship attributes and optional participation need extra constraints.',
'A foreign key must be unique in the referencing table::Many rows may reference the same parent in a many-to-one relationship.||A primary key alone enforces total participation::A non-null foreign key plus referential integrity can enforce some participation constraints; others need additional checks.',
 r'K\to R::A candidate key K functionally determines every attribute in its relation.||FK\subseteq PK_{parent}::Referential integrity constrains referenced values, subject to allowed nulls.')
G('8-queries','SQL uses bags by default and three-valued logic for nulls. Relational algebra commonly uses sets; keep the semantics explicit.',
'Trace FROM/join rows, then WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING and projection; apply DISTINCT only if requested.~For null comparisons use TRUE/FALSE/UNKNOWN; WHERE retains TRUE rows.~For nested queries identify correlated variables and test empty inputs, duplicates and nulls.',
'COUNT(column) counts every row::It excludes nulls in that column; COUNT(*) counts rows.||NOT IN is always interchangeable with NOT EXISTS::A null in the subquery can make NOT IN UNKNOWN, while a suitable NOT EXISTS correlation behaves differently.',
 r'\pi_A(\sigma_p(R))::Select qualifying rows, then project attributes A, under relational-algebra set semantics.||R\bowtie_p S=\sigma_p(R\times S)::Theta join; SQL duplicate counts can differ from set algebra.')
G('8-normalization','Candidate keys, normal forms, losslessness and dependency preservation are separate questions. Compute closure before judging them.',
'Compute attribute closure using all applicable functional dependencies until no attribute changes.~Find minimal keys; identify prime attributes, then test each relevant dependency against 3NF/BCNF definitions.~For a binary split test the intersection dependency for losslessness; for preservation check dependencies inferable within projected schemas.',
'BCNF guarantees dependency preservation::BCNF decomposition may lose an FD that then requires a join to enforce.||A lossless join means no duplicated rows at all::It means joining projections reconstructs exactly the original valid relation, without spurious tuples.',
 r'BCNF:\ X\to Y\ \text{nontrivial}\Rightarrow X\text{ superkey}::Test all implied dependencies, not merely the listed ones.||3NF:\ X\to A\Rightarrow X\text{ superkey or }A\text{ prime}::For each nontrivial dependency with one attribute on the right.||(R_1\cap R_2)\to R_1\ \text{or}\ R_2::Lossless binary decomposition condition under the FDs.')
G('8-storage-index','Index questions often confuse tree order, fanout and leaf capacity. Use the definition supplied by the paper.',
'Compute internal pointer/key capacity and leaf record-entry capacity separately.~Trace lookup, split and redistribution using the specified occupancy convention.~For cost count page reads, consider root caching and distinguish clustered range scans from repeated unclustered record fetches.',
'B+ tree internal keys store every record::Records or record pointers are normally at leaves; internal keys guide traversal.||An index makes every range query cheap::An unclustered range can require many scattered data-page reads, especially when many rows qualify.',
 r'fp+(f-1)k\le B::Internal fanout f with pointer size p, separator size k and available bytes B.||h\approx\lceil\log_f L\rceil+1::Balanced tree levels for L leaves under ideal fanout; occupancy changes bounds.')
G('8-transactions','Conflict serializability, recoverability and locking guarantees measure different properties. Read-from relationships matter.',
'For the precedence graph connect different transactions when conflicting same-item actions occur in order.~Acyclicity gives a conflict-equivalent serial order via topological sorting.~For recovery track who read whose uncommitted write and compare commit order; distinguish basic, strict and rigorous 2PL.',
'Every serializable schedule is recoverable::T2 can read T1’s write and commit before T1 aborts even if the conflict order is serializable.||Every cycle proves lack of view serializability::It disproves conflict serializability; blind writes can permit view-serializable schedules outside that class.',
 r'T_i\to T_j::An earlier conflicting action of Ti precedes an action of Tj on the same item; at least one is a write.||c_i<c_j::Recoverability requires writer Ti to commit before reader Tj commits when Tj reads Ti’s value.')
G('9-layers-switching','Distinguish application messages, transport segments, network packets and link frames. Delay terms are additive only along the modeled path.',
'Identify each layer’s addressing and service, then trace encapsulation across routers.~Separate serialization from propagation, queuing and processing.~For store-and-forward links account for full packet reception before forwarding; for pipelined multiple packets separate first-packet latency from steady throughput.',
'Propagation delay grows with packet size::It depends on distance and propagation speed; serialization depends on packet length and link rate.||Every layer uses the same destination address::Link destination is normally the next hop; network destination usually remains the remote host.',
 r't_{tx}=\frac LR::L bits at R bits/s.||t_{prop}=\frac ds::Distance d and signal speed s in consistent units.||t_{total}=\sum(t_{tx}+t_{prop}+t_{queue}+t_{process})::Sum over modeled hops; queuing must be supplied or modeled.')
G('9-link-layer','CRC, ARQ and shared-medium access solve different problems: detection, reliable transfer and coordinating transmitters.',
'For CRC divide the appended bit polynomial by the generator using XOR, not integer subtraction.~For sliding windows draw sequence-number reuse and outstanding frames before choosing a bound.~For efficiency compare useful transmission time to the full send/ack cycle, then incorporate the allowed window.',
'CRC detects and corrects any corrupted packet::CRC is primarily detection; correction requires additional structure or retransmission.||Selective repeat permits a window as large as the whole sequence space::Old and new packets become ambiguous; its usual maximum is half the sequence space.',
 r'U_{stop}=\frac{T_{frame}}{T_{frame}+2T_{prop}+T_{ack}}::No processing/errors and acknowledgments serialized as shown.||W_{SR}\le2^{m-1},\quad W_{GBN}\le2^m-1::Usual selective-repeat and go-back-N bounds for m-bit numbering and the standard receiver models.')
G('9-routing','Distance-vector and link-state protocols maintain different knowledge. Forwarding uses a routing table; routing computes it.',
'For distance-vector update each destination via every neighbor using that neighbor’s advertised distance.~For link state assemble the topology and run a shortest-path calculation.~For longest-prefix forwarding compare every matching entry and choose the most specific, then use its next hop.',
'Distance-vector updates use the neighbor’s route as instant global truth::Stale advertisements can cause loops and count-to-infinity after failures.||The first matching route always wins::Forwarding normally chooses the longest matching prefix, independent of table listing order.',
 r'D_x(y)=\min_v\{c(x,v)+D_v(y)\}::Bellman equation across neighbors v.||prefix\ length\uparrow\Rightarrow specificity\uparrow::Longest prefix wins among matching routes.')
G('9-ipv4','CIDR masks determine networks and ranges. Conventional subnet host counts have explicit exceptions.',
'Convert the prefix to a mask and AND it with the address to obtain the network.~Set host bits to one for the broadcast address under ordinary subnet semantics.~Check aligned subnet boundaries and aggregation; use /31 point-to-point and /32 conventions only when applicable.',
'Every subnet has 2^h−2 usable hosts::That conventional rule excludes network/broadcast addresses; /31 and /32 have special use cases.||Adjacent equal-size prefixes always aggregate::They must also be aligned siblings; otherwise their common prefix covers extra addresses.',
 r'addresses=2^{32-p}::IPv4 prefix /p.||hosts=2^{32-p}-2::Conventional subnet with network and directed broadcast reserved; not a universal /31 or /32 rule.||network=IP\ \mathrm{AND}\ mask::Bitwise operation, performed on all 32 bits.')
G('9-fragmentation','The IPv4 fragment offset counts payload bytes in units of eight. MTU includes each fragment’s own IP header.',
'Subtract the header from MTU and round nonfinal fragment payload down to a multiple of eight.~Partition the original payload and calculate offsets from cumulative original payload bytes.~Set MF on every fragment except the one containing the original end; all share the original identification.',
'Fragment offset includes preceding headers::It refers to the position in the original payload; per-fragment headers are excluded.||Every fragment including the last must have an eight-byte payload multiple::Nonfinal fragment payloads require that alignment; the final remainder need not.',
 r'D=8\left\lfloor\frac{MTU-H}{8}\right\rfloor::Maximum nonfinal payload for header H bytes.||offset_i=\frac{\text{payload bytes before fragment }i}{8}::Offset field units, not raw bytes.')
G('9-tcp-web','TCP sequence numbers count bytes. Congestion control and receiver flow control impose separate sending limits.',
'Mark the first byte number of each segment and the next expected byte in each cumulative acknowledgment.~Compute outstanding bytes and the minimum of congestion and receive windows.~For RTT/throughput problems state whether handshake, slow start, retransmissions, parallelism and serialization are included.',
'An ACK number identifies the last received byte::It normally identifies the next byte expected; ACK 140 after sequence 100 and 40 payload bytes is consistent.||The advertised receive window is the congestion window::Receiver capacity and network congestion are separate constraints; transmission respects both.',
 r'W_{send}=\min(cwnd,rwnd)::Then subtract outstanding bytes to get permission for new data.||ACK=SEQ+payload\ bytes::For in-order data; SYN and FIN each consume one additional sequence number.||throughput\lesssim\frac W{RTT}::Window bound with W in bits and RTT in seconds, also capped by bottleneck bandwidth.')
G('9-sockets','A socket endpoint and a connection are not the same object. Connection demultiplexing uses endpoints and protocol.',
'Identify local/remote addresses and ports plus TCP or UDP.~For a TCP server distinguish listening socket from accepted connected sockets.~For client calls track name resolution, connection establishment and I/O; reads may return partial byte streams.',
'One TCP read always returns one sender write::TCP preserves ordered bytes, not application message boundaries; framing must be implemented by the application.||TCP and UDP cannot use the same numeric port::Their protocol namespaces are separate, so both can bind the same port number subject to OS binding rules.',
 r'connection=(srcIP,srcPort,dstIP,dstPort,protocol)::A flow key; exact OS socket lookup also depends on binding/context.')
G('9-dns','DNS delegates a namespace and caches answers for their TTL. Recursive and iterative describe how resolution work is assigned.',
'Trace client stub to recursive resolver, then root, TLD and authoritative server as needed.~Apply available cached records before counting queries and delays.~Distinguish A/AAAA addresses, CNAME aliases, NS delegation and MX mail routing; follow aliases where required.',
'Every lookup must contact the root server::Cached delegations and answers can skip most resolution steps.||DNS always uses UDP and one packet::TCP is also used, including some large/truncated responses and zone transfers; transport depends on the scenario.',
 r't_{lookup}=\sum_i RTT_i::Serial cache-miss query exchanges in a simplified delay model; cached and parallel operations change it.')
G('9-http','HTTP performance depends on version, persistence, request ordering and caching. Fix the model before counting RTTs.',
'Identify how many connections are established and which requests can overlap.~Count connection setup and request/first-response latency, then add transmission when specified.~For cache questions distinguish fresh reuse from conditional validation; a 304 carries metadata without the full representation body.',
'Persistent HTTP means all responses arrive simultaneously::Without pipelining or multiplexing, requests can remain sequential on one connection.||All HTTP uses TCP::HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.',
 r'T\approx2RTT+T_{object}::One new TCP connection plus one HTTP request in the simplified no-TLS/no-DNS model.||T\approx RTT+T_{object}::A request on an already established connection under the same assumptions.')
G('10-quantitative','Translate units and bases before calculating. Percentages, averages and work rates depend on what is held fixed.',
'Define unknowns, units and the base quantity for each percentage.~Convert the wording into an equation and solve before evaluating options.~Check boundary cases and approximate magnitude; use substitution into the original condition to reject plausible-looking distractors.',
'Equal percentage rise and fall cancel::Starting at 100, +20% gives 120 and −20% gives 96; the second base has changed.||Average speed is always the mean of two speeds::For equal distances the harmonic mean applies because travel times differ.',
 r'P_{final}=P_0(1+a)(1-b)::Successive fractional increase a and decrease b.||v_{equal\ distance}=\frac{2v_1v_2}{v_1+v_2}::Two equal-distance legs.||\frac1T=\frac1{t_1}+\frac1{t_2}::Constant independent work rates combined on the same job.')
G('10-verbal-reasoning','Choose answers supported by the passage or grammar, separating an assertion from evidence for it.',
'For comprehension state the passage’s main claim and locate the line supporting each candidate.~For inference reject choices that require stronger quantifiers or extra assumptions.~For grammar identify subject, verb, modifier attachment and parallel structure; reread the complete sentence after substitution.',
'A plausible real-world statement must be the inference::The passage may not support it; the answer must follow from the given text.||Some implies most or all::These quantifiers express different strengths; strengthening a statement can turn a valid inference into an unsupported one.',
 r'\text{All }A\text{ are }B:\ A\subseteq B::Does not imply every B is an A.||\text{Some }A\text{ are }B:\ A\cap B\ne\varnothing::An existential claim, not a majority claim.')
